<title>CHINA: China lets nine H-share firms to enjoy 15 pct tax.</title>
<headline>China lets nine H-share firms to enjoy 15 pct tax.</headline>
<dateline>SHANGHAI 1997-03-25</dateline>
<text>
<p>Nine H-share companies, Chinese firms listed in Hong Kong, will continue to enjoy a preferential income tax rate of 15 percent in 1997, the Shanghai Securities News said on Tuesday.</p>
<p>The nine companies, the first batch of firms permitted to list in Hong Kong in 1993, include Shanghai Petrochemical, Tsingtao Brewery, Kunming Machine Tool and Beiren Printing Machinery, the newspaper said.</p>
<p>Maanshan Iron and Steel, Guangzhou Shipyard, Yizheng Chemical Fibre, Dongfang Electrical Machinery and Tianjin Bohai Chemical Industry are also on the list, it said.</p>
<p>China has 26 companies listed in Hong Kong. The other 17 H-share companies pay 33 percent income tax.</p>
</text>
